\f2\i \cf2 Winner, winner, roadkill dinner
\f1\i0 , Chanta thought, then visualised the unfortunate old man, sliced in half, almost kosher style, by the Rose Door. How was he supposed to know it would shut so quickly after he\'92d passed through? \'93I\'92m afraid there\'92s quite a mess down there,\'94 Chanta said bitterly.\
\'93Yes, sir\'97already being cleaned up. A simple matter. It was only a cheap whisky, sir.\'94\
Chanta\'92s shock ate through his muscles like acid. \'93Someone died down there.\'94\
\'93Yes, sir. A homeless Cloudborne,\'94 Fisher answered with professional detachment.\
\'93He was my friend,\'94 Chanta countered trenchantly, the volume of his own voice surprising him. Since when were Elvi indifferent to Cloudborne?\
\'93A friend?\'94 Fisher took a moment to consider. \'93Do you often befriend naked alcoholics?\'94\
\'93I wouldn\'92t say often, exactly\'85\'94\
\'93Would you prefer I performed my duties naked, sir?\'94\
\'93No, no. I\'92m just saying, every life is worth something.\'94\
Fisher frowned. He seemed to be accessing some deep, old thought. \'93We hadn\'92t anticipated you\'92d be a philanthropist.\'94\

\f2\i Fill and throw piss
\f1\i0 , was all that came to Chanta\'92s mind as he tried to parse Fisher\'92s fancy words. \'93There\'92s no need to get personal,\'94 he said, hoping that was enough to cover his ignorance.\
\'93On the contrary,\'94 Fisher answered, as if a new muse was whispering in his thinking ear. \'93We could easily accommodate your value system, sir, and realign your investment portfolio.\'94\
\'93Investment portfolio, you say?\'94\
\'93Yes, sir.\'94\
\'93So, I am\'85 rich, then.\'94\
\'93You might say that.\'94\
\'93What would you say?\'94\
\'93Your fortune, by any economic or ethical measure, is practically unspendable.\'94\
\'93Are you saying I can\'92t spend any of it?\'94\
Fisher suppressed a smile. Or perhaps a grimace. \'93I am saying you could not exhaust it, no matter how hard you tried.\'94\
\'93How disappointing,\'94 Chanta said. \'93Still, I suppose I can afford better whisky.\'94\
\'93We have barrels of aged whisky in the cellar, sir.\'94\
\'93We do?\'94\
\'93It\'92s from your own distillery.\'94\
\'93Where is it? Can I see it now?\'94\
\'93Of course, sir. But perhaps you would prefer a shower and some clean clothes? Unless you would prefer to honour your friend by wearing his blood and\'85 other organic materials around with you.\'94\
Chanta followed Fisher\'92s gaze to his own feet, and saw the drying red-brown stains on his trainers and jeans below the knees. He nodded solemnly.\
Fisher led Chanta from the elevator lobby into a room that seemed to have forgotten it was indoors. Windows stretched the length of the far wall\'97floor to ceiling, wall to wall, no frames, no mullions, just glass pretending it wasn\'92t there. Beyond: the city, the Layer, the sun doing its thing with the atmosphere. Chanta couldn\'92t gauge where the room ended as it curved from sight in both directions. It must have been the size of his entire apartment building. No, bigger. The size of his apartment building if his apartment building had swallowed two other apartment buildings and a car park. Furniture arranged in clusters suggested that important people had important conversations here, though Chanta suspected the furniture cost more than the conversations were worth. Everything was muted, tasteful, expensive in ways that didn\'92t need to announce themselves. The carpet was so thick Chanta\'92s shoes sank into it. The bar along the left inner wall was stocked with bottles that probably had vintage years older than Chanta. Twin staircases curved up to a mezzanine level, presumably leading to rooms even more obscenely comfortable than this one.\
The art on the walls was the kind of art that made you feel stupid for not understanding it, and the longer Chanta looked, the stupider he felt. He suspected the people who bought it didn\'92t understand it either\'97they just knew it was expensive, which was the only understanding that mattered. A monumental chandelier hung from the ceiling like a guillotine dressed in diamonds.\
\'93Nice lounge,\'94 Chanta remarked.\
\'93Oh, this is just the reception room,\'94 Fisher explained.\
\'93Reception room? Like a waiting room?\'94\
\'93No. A place to hold receptions: cocktail parties, board meetings, corporate executions\'85 sometimes all at once.\'94 Fisher smiled in that trademark lopsided ELVIS way. \'93Not that we\'92ve had a good corporate execution for some time.\'94\
Fisher\'92s smile lingered as Chanta glanced nervously at the chandelier.\
\'93There are other more private spaces for sir to relax.\'94
